### Finding stories in it

Finding and checking a story is a separate craft. The [*New York Times* data-training materials](https://github.com/nytimes/data-training), especially *How Not To Be Wrong*, are a good guide. The dictionary's caveats and the provenance sidecar are the inputs to that work.

## Reproduce it

```bash
git clone https://github.com/{{OWNER}}/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}.git
cd {{PROJECT_SLUG}}
uv sync
uv run python -m scripts.pipeline run
```

`run` fetches any originals not in the repository, checks them against the sha256 in `data/original/manifest.json`, and rebuilds `data/processed/`. It stops with an error rather than write empty or partial data. Re-running it on the same originals produces identical files.

<!-- IF:REFRESH -->
## Refresh schedule

`.github/workflows/refresh.yml` reruns the pipeline on a schedule and opens a pull request when anything changed, carrying the audit summary diff for review. Nothing reaches `main` without that review. The git history of `data/audit/summary.md` shows when each refresh changed the data.

<!-- /IF -->
## Corrections

Found a wrong value? Open an issue<!-- IF:PUBLISH --> with the *Data correction* form<!-- /IF --> naming the row and the evidence. Confirmed corrections are fixed in the pipeline and recorded in [`CHANGELOG.md`](CHANGELOG.md) with the affected vintages; earlier values stay in the git history, so anything already cited can be traced.

